    64	{What the system must do when this scenario occurs. State the contract, not the implementation.}
    65	
    66	## Verification
    67	{How we will know the design handles this scenario correctly. Reference test names, manual checks, or metrics.}
    68	
    69	## Related
    70	{Pointer to the design doc and any checklist items that implement this scenario.}
    71	```
    72	
    73	## Coverage field
    74	
    75	The `Coverage` body field (distinct from the base `status` lifecycle field) tracks how well the scenario is handled:
    76	
    77	- **`covered`** — the current design and implementation handle this scenario; verified by a test or check
    78	- **`partial`** — the design intends to handle this but verification is incomplete
    79	- **`uncovered`** — surfaced as a scenario gap but no design addresses it yet (the next Ideation iteration must address)
    80	
    81	The Execution Loop's MEMORIZATION updates the body `Coverage` from `partial` to `covered` when the corresponding verification ships. Base `status` stays `active` until the scenario is superseded.
    82	
    83	## Append-only
    84	
    85	Scenarios accumulate across sessions. Do not delete a scenario when the feature evolves — instead, set status to `uncovered` and document why in the body, or supersede with a more accurate scenario file. Deletion would lose the history of what the feature has been expected to handle. When a scenario is superseded, at Wrap-up it is moved (`git mv`) to `archive/scenarios/{date}-{slug}.md` per the move-on-terminal model (never deleted; full content preserved).

